## Installation
Installation on Windows is a matter of extracting the contents of the download `.zip` to a suitable location. We recommend `{{model.installPath}}`

### Setting up the PATH to include the SaxonC DLLs
If you're intending to use the pre-compiled binaries, like {{model.binariesSentence}},
then you need to ensure that they can find all the DLLs they need on your PATH.

There are several ways to do this. The most permanent is by setting them
through the Environment Variables control panel -- on Windows 11, the simplest
way to locate this is to search for "Environment variables" in the start menu,
or in the Settings panel. "Edit environment variables for your account" should
be what you want, unless you have a clear reason to edit the system environment
variables instead. Assuming you've unpacked to `{{model.installPath}}`,
you'd need to add the following items to the "Path" entry:

    {{ model.SaxonCPackageBinPath }}

    {{ model.SaxonCCorePackageBinPath }}


### Using as a library in your own project
If you're intending to develop against the C++ API, we do not  currently
recommend using the C++ API with the pre-compiled `{{ model.libsaxonc }}`, in case of ABI
conflict. You should compile `{{ model.libsaxonc }}` yourself, and only use the
pre-compiled `{{ model.libsaxoncCore }}`. If you aren't planning to use the
{{ model.binariesSentence }} binaries then you can remove the
entry for `{{ model.SaxonCPackageBinPath }}` from your PATH, which will make it
less likely that your project will find itself loading the wrong DLL.

If you're intending to develop against the C API, then you can use the pre-compiled `{{ model.libsaxonc }}` as well as `{{ model.libsaxoncCore }}`, and you can make use of both PATH entries from the previous step.

In either case, you need to ensure that your build system can locate the header files, from `{{ model.SaxonCCorePackage }}\include` and (if using the pre-compiled `{{ model.libsaxonc }}`) from `{{ model.SaxonCPackage }}\include`.
